# The counters that name things, and what a counter that runs out gives up.
#
# A cache is asked whether it already holds the answer to a question, and
# it answers by comparing a key. Where part of that key is a number a
# counter handed out, the cache is only as trustworthy as the promise
# that the number is never handed out twice. A counter is not state that
# leaks; it is state that REPEATS, which is worse, because the second use
# of a number looks exactly as legitimate as the first and what comes
# back is the wrong answer to a question nobody thought to ask again.
#
# Two things break that promise. A counter kept in virtual memory is
# wound back by restore, and the entries it named are not, so the next
# value is one the cache has already filed something under. And a counter
# of any width eventually runs out; when it starts again, everything it
# named before is indistinguishable from everything it is about to name.
#
# The first is answered by where the counter lives: every counter here
# that names anything is a file-scope static in C, which no restore can
# reach. The second cannot be answered by where it lives, only by what
# happens when it happens -- so that is what each counter is held to
# saying.
#
# The disposition each counter carries:
#
#   mints <function>      The values become part of a cache key. When the
#                         counter runs out, that function is called to
#                         give up everything the old numbers named,
#                         before any number is handed out again. Checked:
#                         the function exists, and the function that
#                         advances the counter both tests it for
#                         exhaustion and calls it.
#
#   mints-signalled <why> The values become part of a cache key, and what
#                         they name is not this module's to give up. The
#                         counter restarts BELOW the last value it handed
#                         out, and the holder of the cache reads that as
#                         the instruction to drop it. The reason must say
#                         who reads it and how. Checked: the function
#                         that advances the counter tests it for
#                         exhaustion.
#
#   refuses <why>         The values become part of a key, and what they
#                         name is not this module's to give up -- an object
#                         a program holds, a cache in another file. There
#                         is no more range to hand out, so whoever asked
#                         for a number is refused rather than answered
#                         with one that means something else. The reason
#                         must say what holds the numbers. Checked: the
#                         function that advances the counter tests it for
#                         exhaustion.
#
#   decides <why>         The value is not part of any key, but a cache
#                         reads it to decide whether what it holds is
#                         still usable. A cache that declines to hold is
#                         not obviously broken and costs more than no
#                         cache at all, so the reason must say what the
#                         reading decides.
#
#   counts <why>          It names nothing and decides nothing: a total,
#                         a limit, a length or a nesting depth. Running
#                         out costs an inaccurate number, never a wrong
#                         answer. The reason must say which it is.
#
# The population is derived, not listed: every file-scope static in
# src/lib whose declaration carries no * and whose type is integral --
# pointers belong to tests/library_statics.golden, which speaks for a
# different question -- and which is ADVANCED somewhere in its own file,
# by ++, --, += or -=. Advancing is the mechanism, not the name: a
# counter called _serial_next and a counter called n are the same thing
# to a cache, and deriving on the spelling would have found the ones
# already thought about and missed exactly the one nobody had.
#
#   <file>  <name>  <disposition>  <reason or function>

src/lib/xpost_op_font.c      _fontserial_next     mints  xpost_mask_cache_clear
src/lib/xpost_dev_generic.c  _region_serial_next  mints  _region_memo_flush

src/lib/xpost_op_path.c      _form_serial_next    mints-signalled  the form cache lives in
  graphicsdict beside .formlast, the last serial it was given; a serial no greater than
  that one is the counter having restarted, and .formcachedrop gives the cache up

src/lib/xpost_interpreter.c  nextid               refuses  a context identifier is what a
  context-typed object a program holds names its context by, and what the resolved
  graphics state is cached against, and neither is this module's to give up: the object
  is the program's and the cache is another file's. It restarts with the interpreter
  instance, which is where both of those go too, so the numbers of one instance name
  nothing of another; inside an instance a second use of a number would make a stale
  object name a live context, so the end of the range refuses the fork instead

src/lib/xpost_op_path.c      _clipcuts            decides  the count of clips applied,
  read before a form's drawing is captured and again after, so a capture made under a clip
  that changed part way through is not offered to a later placement

src/lib/xpost_dev_generic.c  _region_memo_clock   counts  the order the memo's entries were
  last used, so the one to give up is the one used longest ago; a wrap costs a poor choice
  of victim, never a wrong region
src/lib/xpost_font.c         gcache_bsize         counts  the bytes of glyph raster held
src/lib/xpost_font.c         gcache_csize         counts  the glyphs held
src/lib/xpost_font.c         gcache_nstate        counts  the live entries in the per-face
  transform table, which is an extent, not a name
src/lib/xpost_font.c         _xpost_font_faces    counts  the faces open at this moment,
  which is what xpost_font_faces_held answers
src/lib/xpost_op_font.c      face_cache_n         counts  the slots of the face cache in
  use, which is an extent

src/lib/xpost_op_font.c      face_cache_clock     counts  the order the face cache's
  entries were last asked for, so the one to give up is the one asked for longest ago; a
  wrap costs a poor choice of victim, never a wrong face
src/lib/xpost_garbage.c      _xpost_garbage_work_n  counts  the entries on the collector's
  work list
src/lib/xpost_operator.c     _xpost_noops         counts  the rows the operator table holds,
  which is an extent: it is bounded by the table's own size where it is advanced, and reset
  with the table each context allocates, so the opcodes taken from it do not outlive it
src/lib/xpost_interpreter.c  _contexts_created    counts  how many contexts this process
  has built, read only to tell the first from the rest
src/lib/xpost_main.c         _xpost_at_quit_n     counts  the functions registered to be
  called when the library goes down
src/lib/xpost_main.c         _xpost_init_count    counts  how deep the library's init and
  quit are nested, so the last quit is the one that takes it down
  waiting, which is reported and never compared
src/lib/xpost_name.c         _name_lookups        counts  the strings offered to the name
  mechanism that had to be looked up in the tree, which is a total of work done; it
  saturates rather than wrapping, and running out costs an understated total, never a
  wrong name

# ---- obligations ----
#
# Two invariants the counters above rest on which are not properties of a
# counter, and so have nowhere else to be written down. Each is held to
# the mechanism that enforces it, so that removing the enforcement fails
# here rather than going quiet.
#
#   colocated <file> <dict> <a> <b>
#       Two names must live in the same dictionary. A serial and the
#       cache it keys are wound back by restore together only if one
#       restore reaches both; put them in different dictionaries and the
#       cache outlives the number that says which entries are whose.
#
#   detects <file> <remembered> <dropper>
#       A counter that gives up nothing itself, and restarts below the
#       last value it handed out so that somebody else does, is only
#       safe if that somebody exists. The remembered value and the
#       giving up must sit together, so that a signal sent has a reader.
#
#   releases <file> <function> <field>
#       A cache keyed on the address of a thing must give up its entries
#       where that thing is released, not at the teardown. An address is
#       reissued freely within one run, so entries surviving the release
#       of what they name are entries a later, unrelated thing collides
#       with.

obligation  colocated  data/init.ps          graphicsdict          .formcache  .formlast
obligation  detects    data/init.ps          .formlast             .formcachedrop
obligation  releases   src/lib/xpost_font.c  xpost_font_face_free  k1
